      {
        "mass_number": 48,
        "symbol": "48Ca",
        "common_name": "Calcium-48",
        "is_stable": false,
        "half_life_formatted": "56.0 Eyear",
        "half_life_seconds": null,
        "decay_modes": [],
        "abundance_percent": 18.7,
        "nuclear_spin": "0",
        "binding_energy_per_nucleon_mev": null,
        "narrative": "Calcium-48 is a scarce isotope of calcium containing 20 protons and 28 neutrons. It makes up 0.187% of natural calcium by mole fraction. Although it is unusually neutron-rich for such a light nucleus, its beta decay is extremely hindered, and so the only radioactive decay pathway that it has been observed to undergo is the extremely rare double beta decay (2\u03b2). Its half-life is about 5.6\u00d71019 years (which is within the normal range for double beta) so for all practical purposes it can be treated as stable. One cause of this unusual stability is that 20 and 28 are both magic numbers, making 48Ca a \"doubly magic\" nucleus.\n\nSince 48Ca is both practically stable and neutron-rich, it is a valuable starting material for the production of new nuclei in particle accelerators, both by fragmentation and by fusion reactions with other nuclei, for example in the discoveries of the five heaviest known elements, from flerovium to oganesson (atomic numbers 114 through 118). Heavier nuclei generally require a greater fraction of neutrons for maximum stability, so neutron-rich starting materials are necessary.",
